  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through Saturday)\nIssued at 300 PM CDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nA period of unsettled weather with increasing rain and\nthunderstorm chances still looks to impact southeast New Mexico \nand much of west Texas late Tuesday through Friday. An upper- \nlevel low will slowly pivot from Nevada/Utah/northern Arizona \nTuesday and Tuesday night before lifting toward the Rocky\nMountains Wednesday and Wednesday night. The upper-level ridge\naxis will remain oriented over eastern portions of Texas and into\nthe Gulf Coast states through midweek. Tropical moisture associated\nwith tropical cyclone Simon located off the coast of western \nMexico early this week will be drawn northeastward into much of \nsouthwest and west Texas and far southeast New Mexico within the \nsouthwesterly flow pattern aloft between these features both days.\nPrecipitable water values will be near or slightly above \nclimatological maxima for mid-October from the Big Bend into the \nPermian Basin, with values ranging between 1.6 and 1.9 inches per \nsome of the latest high resolution (RRFS) and short term forecast \nguidance (GFS). Shortwave impulses embedded within the southwesterly\nflow aloft will translate across our forecast area Tuesday afternoon\nthrough Wednesday night. We initially expect scattered to locally\nnumerous (30-60 percent) showers and thunderstorms to develop \nfrom the Big Bend into the Davis Mountains/Trans Pecos and Permian\nBasin late Tuesday afternoon into Tuesday night. CAPE values up to\n1000-1500 J/KG and deep layer shear values of 25-35 kt will\nsupport a few strong to severe storms capable of producing\nlocalized damaging wind gusts and possibly some hail Tuesday\nafternoon and evening. The deep moisture will also promote heavy\nrainfall within the stronger storms that develop, so we will also\nbe monitoring for localized instances of flash flooding. A more\npotent shortwave trough looks to translate over our forecast area\nWednesday and Wednesday night. Increased ascent and the deeply\nmoist airmass should allow for the development of another round of\nscattered to numerous showers and thunderstorms from the Big Bend\ninto the Trans Pecos, far southeast New Mexico Plains, and the\nPermian Basin Wednesday afternoon through Wednesday night. The\nbest chance of storms looks to be along and east of a Seminole,\nWink, Pecos/Balmorhea, Marfa, Presidio line. Heavy rainfall and\nlocalized flash flooding will once again become a concern given \nthe high PWAT airmass. Highs Tuesday will remain above normal with\nreadings in the upper 80s to lower 90s over most places. Highs on\nWednesday trend slightly cooler in the mid 80s to around 90\ndegrees. \n\nThe upper-trough continues to lift over the central Plains on\nThursday, while another trough pinches over the Desert Southwest.\nUpper-level ridging meanwhile remains oriented from east Texas to\nthe Gulf Coast. The plume of tropical moisture remains oriented \nfrom the Big Bend to the Permian Basin Thursday. A cold front is\nstill on track to push into our forecast area Thursday night.\nPotential for heavy rainfall increases along this boundary\nThursday night into Friday. The latest ECMWF ensemble guidance has\nincreased the probability of rainfall >1\" to 40-60% from the\nPermian Basin to the Trans Pecos and Big Bend ahead of along the\ncold front Thursday into Thursday night. Rain chances continue on\nFriday before shifting west Friday night. Much cooler and drier\nweather can be expected this weekend with lows falling into the\n40s to lower 50s. Highs on Saturday only reach into the 60s (50s\nhigher elevation) and slightly warmer in the 60s to lower 70s on\nSunday.",
